  "title": "What is biblical wisdom?",
  "aliases": [
    "biblical wisdom question",
    "biblical wisdom in the bible",
    "biblical wisdom overview"
  ],
  "summary": "Biblical wisdom is the skill of living well before God: listening to instruction, choosing trustworthy paths, and acting with integrity and care. Proverbs connects wisdom with reverence for God; Job says it includes turning from evil; Psalm 1 contrasts two ways of life; and Jesus compares hearing his words with building on a solid foundation. Wisdom is meant to shape choices, not just provide information.",
  "historical_context": "The cited passages present wisdom in several settings rather than tracing one historical origin. Proverbs 1 frames its teaching as instruction for learning; Psalm 1 portrays a person reflecting on God’s teaching; Job 28 asks where wisdom can be found; and Ecclesiastes closes by urging reverence for God and attention to one’s actions. These contexts show how biblical texts teach wisdom through instruction, poetry, reflection, and conclusion.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "The examples in this record provide the relevant setting: Proverbs offers instruction, Psalm 1 compares two paths, Job 28 reflects on wisdom’s limits, and Matthew 7 uses a building story to distinguish hearing from doing. No comparative source is recorded for broader claims about Israel and neighboring cultures, so the explanation stays with these passages.",
